<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malcolm III of Scotland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1093">1093</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Malcolm Canmore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malcolm III, called Canmore, was king of Scots from 1058 to 1093. He killed Macbeth in 1057 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lulach">Lulach</a> in 1058, and married first Ingibiorg and then the English princess Margaret, whose sons founded the line of later Scottish kings. He raided northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> several times and was killed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alnwick">Alnwick</a> in 1093.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malcolm-iii-of-scotland.jpg" alt="Malcolm III of Scotland" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Bannermann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malcolm3Canmore.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Malcolm was the son of Duncan I, who became king in late 1034 and was killed in battle in Moray on 15 August 1040 by a force under Macbeth. Malcolm and his brother Donald were probably children. Later tradition says they were sent away for safety, Malcolm to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, but neither the place of exile nor the fact of it is certain; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, Ireland and Strathclyde have also been suggested. Later tradition, attested by the fifteenth century, made his mother the daughter of the miller of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forteviot">Forteviot</a>.</p><p>Chroniclers report that Malcolm killed Macbeth at Lumphanan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeenshire">Aberdeenshire</a> on 15 August 1057. Macbeth's stepson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lulach">Lulach</a> was crowned at Scone, probably on 8 September 1057, and was killed by Malcolm &quot;by treachery&quot; near Huntly on 23 April 1058.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Malcolm's kingdom did not cover all of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>: many islands and the land north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-oykel">River Oykel</a> were Scandinavian, and south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a> lay independent or semi-independent realms. In 1061 the Scots plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a>. In 1068 Malcolm gave asylum to English exiles fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William of Normandy</a>, among them Edgar Ætheling and his sisters Margaret and Cristina. By the end of 1070 he had married Margaret, later Saint Margaret. Their sons were given English royal names, including Edward and Edmund, and also Alexander and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, the later kings Alexander I and David I.</p><p>In 1072 William came north, and at Abernethy Malcolm became his man, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> puts it, and handed over his eldest son Duncan as a hostage. Raids into Northumbria followed. In 1080 William's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-curthose">Robert Curthose</a> marched north and Malcolm again made peace. In 1091 Malcolm besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a>, then withdrew before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england">William Rufus</a>'s army and again acknowledged English overlordship. A tradition in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkneyinga-saga">Orkneyinga saga</a> gives him an earlier marriage to Ingibiorg, widow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thorfinn-the-mighty">Thorfinn Sigurdsson</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In August 1093 Malcolm travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england">William Rufus</a> refused to negotiate and insisted that English barons decide the dispute. Malcolm returned north and invaded Northumbria, a ravaging seen as harsh even by the standards of the time. On 13 November 1093 Robert de Mowbray's forces confronted him on his march north, and Robert's steward Morel of Bamburgh killed him. His son Edward was mortally wounded, and Margaret is said to have died soon after hearing the news.</p><p>His brother Donald III succeeded him. His body was buried at Tynemouth Priory and later moved north, probably to Dunfermline Abbey. In 1250, after Margaret was canonised, both were reburied beside the altar there. Malcolm secured the line that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> until the late thirteenth century, and he is a major character in Shakespeare's Macbeth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malcolm III of Scotland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>King Macbeth of Scotland was killed at Lumphanan in Aberdeenshire by forces of <b>Malcolm Canmore</b>, Duncan's son.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-15">15 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Siward, Earl of Northumbria, invading Scotland in support of <b>Malcolm Canmore</b>'s claim to the throne, defeated Macbeth, King of Scotland, somewhere north of the Firth of Forth.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-27">27 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The house of Moray clashed repeatedly with Norse earls of Orkney and with the line of <b>Malcolm III</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-moray">Kingdom of Moray</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alnwick">Alnwick</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=57,-5&amp;year=1083" aria-label="Scotland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4442 230 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Malcolm III of Scotland"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4892 500h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4892 500h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=57,-5&amp;year=1083">What was here in 1083? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
